    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (BUILD SUCCESS). \n\nThe change is complete. Here's a summary of what I implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `capacityWarning` flag is now returned `true` when an owner's city already holds 40\u201349 owners (approaching the hard limit of 50), following the same persisted-field pattern used by the existing `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacity.java`** \u2014 After the unchanged hard rejection at `count >= 50`, it now stamps `owner.setCapacityWarning(count >= 40)`. Since the throw already short-circuits at \u226550, this cleanly yields `true` only for the 40\u201349 band and `false` below 40.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `capacityWarning` boolean field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with `isCapacityWarning()`/`setCapacityWarning()`, mirroring `bulkSignupWarning`.\n\n3. **Schema files** (postgres, hsqldb, h2, mysql) \u2014 Added `capacity_warning BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` to the `owners` table.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the `capacityWarning` read-only boolean property to the owner DTO schema (which is code-generated into `OwnerDto`, and mapped by name automatically via MapStruct).\n\nThe hard 409 rejection at 50 owners is unchanged. The full test suite (including the new `coreWarnsWhenApproachingCapacity` acceptance test) passes: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
